the state of public education is under  scrutiny once again as another major  teacher strike gets underway seattle's  first day of school is canceled with  teachers hitting the picket line today  they want more pay they also want better  conditions for their students l thomas  of our seattle station cairo has more on  this story  i'm kind of sad because i was excited  for my first day of school i understand  why they're doing it i mean teachers  don't get paid that much better pays  smaller class sizes and mental health  resources are all issues on the  bargaining table for seattle public  school teachers sps has given us no  choice jennifer motter is the union  president  we can't continue  that same status quo it's unsustainable  it's leading to educator burnout where  students needs are not being met  it's a sentiment shared nationwide a  recent survey of public school teachers  found that 90  said burnout is a serious problem and  there are at least 280 thousand fewer  public school teachers working now than  before the pandemic  this is unprecedented the level of  trauma that we're seeing in our  populations and just the amount of work  that we have to do now  after coming back from the pandemic we  take this moment seriously the district  superintendent said negotiators are  working hard to come to a resolution  seeking agreement should not result in  extra stress for our students family  staff and community teachers agree but  argue the strike is a necessary step  toward putting the community first  we understand the impact that has on  families  i wouldn't be here if what the district  was proposing was not unconscionable  the district did propose  percentage-based wage hikes for teachers  who called those increases significantly  smaller than their demands a  spokesperson said the district was  disappointed with the decision to strike  but added that it was still committed to  negotiating on a new contract vlad  well thomas great reporting thank you  very much
 

